WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:07.350
One year ago here in Mersin, smugglers' boats
were still casting off, night after night.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.580
But their business fell away after the EU
and Turkey signed the Refugee Deal.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.890
This smuggler tells us that only an average
of twenty people a month were willing to pay

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:21.670
up to 600 dollars for the clandestine passage
to Europe.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:26.970
Now that disagreements between the EU and
Turkey have broken out, things have changed

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.020
again.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.500
"Police controls and coast guard controls
have been more relaxed here the past couple

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.500
of days.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.980
And we are suddenly receiving more requests
from refugees again.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:43.589
It is very noticeable."

00:00:43.589 --> 00:00:48.690
Have Turkey's threats to scrap the Refugee
Deal renewed Syrian refugees' hopes, here

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.300
in Mersin?

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.030
The reactions of the 300,000 refugees living
in this part of the city are mixed.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:01.359
This couple from northern Syria does not want
to stay in Turkey:

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:04.570
"We are living from day to day.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.110
We work in the fields once in awhile.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.520
But we do not speak the language.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.270
We are foreigners here."

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.690
"Back in Syria, I sold everything.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:18.320
I wanted to get myself and my family away
from here."

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.850
Other Syrians will remain in Turkey, for now.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.570
They are more interested in returning home,
than moving on to Europe.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.810
They are critical of Turkey's current political
stance towards them.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.910
"Turkey is using us refugees as bargaining
chips with Europe.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:35.910
That's not good.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.530
Because I would prefer to return to my home
country, as soon as it's possible."

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:44.460
Mersin's fishermen don't understand.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.450
They say that most refugees have a good life
here, and that is why the smugglers' boats

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.830
stopped setting sail from here a long time
ago.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.420
"Two years ago, they still had no work.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.980
But now many of them have started a business.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.310
They are often better off than us!"

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:05.060
Even if the Refugee Deal between Turkey and
the EU does collapse, it is unlikely that

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.289
tens of thousands of those seeking protection
would leave for the EU.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:12.590
Because they are well-aware that with the
Balkan route "closed," there's very little

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.730
chance of getting any further than Greece.

